# The RP2 bootloader is not a disk, so stop probing it like one.
#
# flash-rp2040 resets the ReTool into the RP2 bootloader and flashes it over
# PICOBOOT. That bootloader exposes USB Mass Storage alongside PICOBOOT, so the
# kernel registers a block device we never intend to mount, udev runs blkid
# against it, and rp2040_flash then reboots the chip out of the bootloader while
# that probe is still in flight. The reads fail because the device went away:
#
#   I/O error, dev sdb, sector 297 op 0x0:(READ)
#   Buffer I/O error on dev sdb1, logical block 296, async page read
#
# Harmless, but it reads exactly like a failing USB drive on every first boot,
# and it cost real time once already (Rebuild #94).
#
# Numbered 55 so it is processed before 60-persistent-storage.rules, which is
# what honours both of these flags - the second one skips the blkid probe that
# does the actual reading.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2e8a", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0003", ENV{UDEV_DISABLE_PERSISTENT_STORAGE_RULES_FLAG}="1", ENV{UDEV_DISABLE_PERSISTENT_STORAGE_BLKID_FLAG}="1", ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}="1", OPTIONS+="nowatch"
